<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stamford Raffles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1781">1781</a>–<a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Thomas Stamford Raffles, Sir Stamford Raffles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Stamford Raffles was a British official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. He served at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a> from 1805 and was Lieutenant-Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> from 1811 to 1816 during the British occupation, when he reformed land revenue, deposed or reduced Javanese rulers and wrote a History of Java. As Lieutenant-Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengkulu">Bencoolen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> from 1818 he founded the British settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in 1819.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles.jpg" alt="Stamford Raffles" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Francis Joseph · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Francis_Joseph_-_Sir_Thomas_Stamford_Bingley_Raffles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
